      {
        "id": "lem-bowl-shaped-loss-has-centered-lipschitz-minorants",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Bounded bowl-shaped losses have Gaussian-centered Lipschitz minorants",
        "statement": "Assume AC. Let $L:\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,M]$ be Borel measurable, symmetric and quasi-convex, with $M>0$. For integers $N\\ge2$ and $m\\ge1$, put $\\delta=M/N$, $C_k=\\overline{\\{x:L(x)\\le k\\delta\\}}$ and\n$$g_{N,m}(x)=\\delta\\sum_{k=1}^{N-1}\\min(1,m\\,d(x,C_k)).$$\nFor $C_k=\\varnothing$ the corresponding summand is defined to be one, and for $C_k=\\mathbb R^d$ it is zero. Then $0\\le g_{N,m}\\le L$, and $g_{N,m}$ is bounded and Lipschitz. For every nondegenerate Gaussian $H\\sim N_d(b,S)$,\n$$\\inf_a E g_{N,m}(a-H)=E g_{N,m}(H-b),\\qquad\n\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\lim_{m\\to\\infty}E g_{N,m}(H-b)=E L(H-b).$$\nEach summand is symmetric and quasi-convex; no quasi-convexity of their sum is asserted.",
        "deps": [
          "lem-distance-to-set-is-lipschitz",
          "def-metric-bounded-diameter",
          "def-infimum",
          "thm-infimum-property",
          "cor-triangle-inequality-for-inner-product-norm",
